Yes, you can achieve this by using Jira's built-in features and creating a custom validation rule for your workflow. Here's how:

1. Create a custom field of type "Checklist" in your issue type. Let's call it "Required Tasks".
2. Add all the tasks that need to be completed before moving the issue to the next column as items in the checklist field.
3. In your workflow, create a new transition between the current column and the target column (the one you want to block the issue from moving to).
4. Add a custom validation rule to the transition. To do this, go to "Workflows" > "Transitions", click on the transition you just created, then scroll down to the "Validation" section.
5. Click on the "Add Validation Rule" button, and select "Custom Validation Rule".
6. Give your validation rule a name, and add the following code to the "Validate" field:
```groovy
import com.atlassian.jira.component.ComponentAccessor;
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.Issue;
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.fields.ChecklistField;

public class AllTasksCompletedValidationRule implements ValidationRule {
    public boolean validate(Transition transition, Issue issue) {
        ChecklistField checklistField = (ChecklistField) issue.getField("Required Tasks");
        return checklistField.getValue() == null || checklistField.getValue().size() == 0;
    }
}
```
This code checks if the "Required Tasks" field is empty or contains no items. If it does, the transition will be blocked, and the issue will not be allowed to move to the target column.

7. Save your workflow and test your validation rule by moving an issue through the workflow. If all required tasks are completed, the issue should not be able to move to the target column.

Remember to adjust the code according to your specific requirements and field names.

