To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in your Next.js application running on WordPress, you need to set up proper CORS configuration. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Install the required packages:

First, make sure you have `cors` package installed in your Next.js project. You can install it using npm or yarn:

```bash
npm install cors
```

or

```bash
yarn add cors
```

2. Configure CORS in your Next.js app:

Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages` directory, and add the following code:

```javascript
// pages/cors.js
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'HEAD'],
  origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com', // Replace with your WordPress site URL
});

export default cors.handle;
```

Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site. This configuration allows GET and HEAD requests from your WordPress site to your Next.js app.

3. Add CORS to your Next.js server configuration:

In your `next.config.js` file, add the following code to configure CORS for your server:

```javascript
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  // ...
  serverMiddleware: [cors.handle],
};
```

This will ensure that all incoming requests to your Next.js server are handled by the CORS middleware.

4. Update your WordPress site's settings:

To allow requests from your Next.js app to your WordPress site, you need to update your WordPress site's settings. Go to your WordPress dashboard, click on "Settings" > "General", and add your Next.js app's domain or IP address to the "Allowed Hosts" field. Save your changes.

Now, your Next.js app should be able to communicate with your WordPress site without any CORS errors.

