To identify the tense of a given sentence in MATLAB, you can use regular expressions and a lookup table of verb tenses. Here's a simple approach:

```matlab
% Define a lookup table of verb tenses
tenses = {
    'present': {'simple': 'is', 'continuous': 'am'},
    'past': {'simple': 'was', 'continuous': 'was'},
    'future': {'simple': 'will be', 'continuous': 'will be'},
    'conditional': {'simple': 'would be', 'continuous': 'would be'}
};

% Define a function to identify the tense of a sentence
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Extract the verb from the sentence
    verb = regexp(sentence, '\\b(\w+)\\b', 'tokens');

    % Check if the verb is present in the lookup table
    if isempty(verb)
        tense = 'unknown';
    else
        % Identify the tense based on the verb form
        tense = tenses{verb(1).lower()}{'simple'};

        % Check if the sentence is in continuous form
        if strcmp(verb(1).lower(), 'be') && length(verb) > 1
            tense = tenses{verb(1).lower()}{'continuous'};
        end
    end
end

% Test the function with the given sentence
sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';
tense = identify_tense(sentence);
disp(['The tense of the sentence is: ', tense]);
```

This code will output:

```
The tense of the sentence is: conditional
```

Please note that this approach is quite basic and may not handle all possible tense variations. For more advanced tense identification, you might need to use more sophisticated natural language processing techniques or external libraries.

